WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.010 --> 00:00:07.050
The rise of the state is a key point in

00:00:07.719 --> 00:00:10.708
the the evolution of human society.

00:00:11.919 --> 00:00:16.829
Most of the research and certainly in the social sciences has been

00:00:17.650 --> 00:00:25.170
directed at what happened. But both...in a sense the why and how questions have not been tackled with the same level of sophistication

00:00:25.630 --> 00:00:31.020
as the what question and this project wants to explore and see if they are

00:00:31.450 --> 00:00:34.709
regularities in the in the evolution of early states.

00:00:34.719 --> 00:00:42.209
But also we want to know why in similar circumstances in other parts of the world at different times and places the state

00:00:42.489 --> 00:00:44.489
did not arise.

00:00:48.719 --> 00:00:50.719
The earliest states

00:00:50.940 --> 00:00:52.079
probably

00:00:52.079 --> 00:00:53.910
degan, in what's now

00:00:53.910 --> 00:01:00.349
referred to as lower Mesopotamia in modern, modern day particularly center in in Iraq and

00:01:00.600 --> 00:01:03.019
in neighboring areas. Probably around

00:01:04.830 --> 00:01:08.749
3,000 BCE or a little earlier. I think one of the fascinating

00:01:09.300 --> 00:01:15.469
topics for us in this project is to look at what are often referred to as primary states. That is states

00:01:15.810 --> 00:01:19.040
beginning in areas where there are no predecessors.

00:01:19.040 --> 00:01:24.019
And this probably happened at least six or seven times in in world history. I think

00:01:24.750 --> 00:01:27.169
legitimately, we can say that a state

00:01:27.869 --> 00:01:30.258
involves the beginnings of

00:01:31.500 --> 00:01:32.940
large-scale

00:01:32.940 --> 00:01:39.139
bureaucracy and administration and in different levels in the hierarchy of administration and the

00:01:40.020 --> 00:01:42.710
control of such

00:01:44.190 --> 00:01:45.780
hierarchy by

00:01:45.780 --> 00:01:47.809
an elite who also has

00:01:48.539 --> 00:01:54.379
some kind, of course, of power via standing army or other ways to enforce the

00:01:54.840 --> 00:01:57.170
kind of the rules and laws that this bureaucracy

00:01:58.020 --> 00:02:02.359
comes up with. And what's fascinating when you look at these pristine states

00:02:02.360 --> 00:02:06.889
and then other examples of secondary states that arise, there seems to be

00:02:07.590 --> 00:02:09.270
significant patterning in

00:02:09.270 --> 00:02:15.859
the way, they arise. So what we really wanted to find out are their commonalities that we can actually come up

00:02:15.900 --> 00:02:19.340
not only with a clear model and an explanation of

00:02:20.069 --> 00:02:26.598
why and how they arose but literally if we can actually come up with a quantifiable model

00:02:26.819 --> 00:02:34.309
that's predictive. Our current understandings in most general form of the rise of early states, is that they

00:02:35.670 --> 00:02:37.670
develop in the context of

00:02:38.160 --> 00:02:42.799
competing chiefdoms that is small, smaller scale groups

00:02:43.260 --> 00:02:47.330
in a given landscape who are competing for resources...

00:02:48.390 --> 00:02:53.690
for labor and so on and it's in this competitive environment

00:02:54.090 --> 00:02:57.119
that the kind of the next step that allows one of these

00:02:57.670 --> 00:03:01.320
competing groups to succeed and ultimately take over

00:03:02.290 --> 00:03:10.020
the others. That's how states arise, part of it is is military skills and mobilization of

00:03:11.350 --> 00:03:13.350
significant, well

00:03:14.770 --> 00:03:19.169
cooperating groups, but also the ability through trade

00:03:19.690 --> 00:03:23.039
you need to have to support both the elite

00:03:24.280 --> 00:03:27.479
themselves and also the elites ability to to mobilize

00:03:28.300 --> 00:03:33.149
forces, and then the build up of administrative organization to

00:03:33.940 --> 00:03:38.609
effectively control areas that might have been taken over by military conquest.

00:03:38.610 --> 00:03:40.610
It's an effect the the

00:03:40.750 --> 00:03:47.550
higher level of organization that one of the competing groups is able to achieve that gives it the ability to

00:03:49.450 --> 00:03:54.149
out-compete if you will of the the other chiefdoms. What are the key variables?

00:03:55.030 --> 00:03:56.440
clearly

00:03:56.440 --> 00:03:58.060
conflict,

00:03:58.060 --> 00:04:00.839
competition for resources are important.

00:04:00.840 --> 00:04:06.989
But there may well be a host of others in in the realm of ideologies in the in the realm of politics

00:04:07.630 --> 00:04:09.630
You know let alone

00:04:09.640 --> 00:04:15.989
economics, and also the general role that ecology and landscape may play as well. That is a

00:04:16.810 --> 00:04:22.979
large complex adaptive system in each case. In today's world one of the exciting things for

00:04:23.590 --> 00:04:25.030
archaeologists are the

00:04:25.030 --> 00:04:27.690
quantity of terrific data from a number of

00:04:27.850 --> 00:04:30.239
individual sites or regions throughout the world.

00:04:30.340 --> 00:04:37.590
What we've lacked and what our Templeton Foundation project is going to tackle is trying to compare these many cases on a

00:04:37.660 --> 00:04:44.760
global basis and see if we can find common processes that led to the rise of the state and early cities throughout time and space.

00:04:45.040 --> 00:04:50.249
Part of our project is to build a database on all the available

00:04:51.220 --> 00:04:55.499
archaeological data about early state development that we can put our hands on. A

00:04:55.930 --> 00:05:02.990
number of SFI scientists have been looking at different aspects of the rise of the early states and the cities combining

00:05:03.570 --> 00:05:04.980
perspectives from

00:05:04.980 --> 00:05:08.480
cultural anthropology, from archaeology, from history

00:05:08.480 --> 00:05:13.909
but also using modeling from biology, from physics and a host of

00:05:14.490 --> 00:05:16.490
disciplines. Obviously looking at new

00:05:16.590 --> 00:05:18.889
methods particularly computer simulations.

00:05:18.890 --> 00:05:26.210
And it's this project will bring all of these different approaches together. Where we hope will be a really productive and important result.

00:05:27.060 --> 00:05:33.380
Why study the state? What's so important? And the answer is really twofold. One is its inherently interesting.

00:05:33.380 --> 00:05:41.059
I think historically to understand how states and cities first arose but just as importantly and I think for many of us we feel it

00:05:41.400 --> 00:05:48.650
understanding and explaining the rise of states and cities will provide us with new perspectives on the problems facing the world today.

00:05:52.639 --> 00:05:54.639
 

